  "transcript": "# HMRC’s anti-fraud intervention on child benefit\n\n## What the intervention was and HMRC’s opening admission\n> HMRC used travel data to find likely ineligible child benefit claims, apologised after the first roll-out caused harm, and accepted NAO recommendations.\n\nChair: Child benefit supports millions and cost £13.3bn in 2024–25. HMRC used Home Office travel data in 2025 to find people who may have moved abroad and were no longer eligible.\nJohn-Paul Marks: We accept the NAO’s findings and have accepted all recommendations. The pilot showed strong results, but the first roll-out made mistakes. I apologised to affected customers, we reinstated payments and offered redress.\nRupert Lowe: The pilot seemed to work: six in 10 flagged claims were in error. But decisions changed in roll-out—removing an upfront PAYE check and altering forms—and that’s where things went wrong. Who authorised those changes?\nJohn-Paul Marks: I’m accountable as HMRC chief executive. Those operational changes were made to scale quickly, but they were outside our risk tolerance. We reversed the changes, put PAYE checks back, added a grace period and simplified evidence requirements.\nRob Malcomson: The pilot was successful but highlights the need for guardrails. The Digital Economy Act review board and the PSFA will use this learning across Government.\n\n\n## Pilot to roll-out: how the changes caused harm\n> Changes made when scaling up—especially removing checks and asking for too much evidence—led to thousands of eligible claimants having payments suspended.\n\nRupert Lowe: The NAO said key decisions were made at inappropriate levels. Were there minutes of the workshop where this happened, and who is responsible going forward?\nJohn-Paul Marks: I will check the paperwork, but operational teams acted to scale innovation. Now Myrtle’s directorate runs the business-as-usual process and Cerys is the policy owner—there’s clearer senior accountability.\nSarah Olney: You described the disruption as a 'bump in the road'. For families, paused child benefit can mean real hardship. Why was customer impact not considered when those roll-out changes were made?\nJohn-Paul Marks: No intent to cause harm. It was poor judgement to scale without re-testing at volume. We paused when problems were spotted, reinstated payments, apologised and offered redress.\nMyrtle Lloyd: From pilot to scale we added a 'hypercare' phase: when we now roll out we do close follow-up calls, 100% quality checks and watch colleague capability so customers aren’t left without help.\nSarah Olney: Forms initially asked for up to 73 items including school or GP letters. That was disproportionate. Have those requirements changed?\nMyrtle Lloyd: Yes. We reduced the questions to about 15 and now ask for two pieces of evidence—return travel and bank transactions—submitted digitally where possible.\n\n\n## Scale of impact, reinstatement and redress\n> About 9,000 ineligible claims were stopped saving £50m, but thousands of eligible claimants were suspended; most payments were reinstated quickly and redress offered, though compensation paid was small.\n\nRupert Lowe: You stopped around 9,000 people who were ineligible, saving over £50m, yet 61% of the initial 23,794 inquiries were actually eligible. That’s a high false-positive rate.\nJohn-Paul Marks: The pilot non-compliance rate was ~60%. First roll-out was lower—about 38–39%—because of the changes we later reversed. In hypercare more recently, non-compliance rose again closer to pilot levels.\nChair: NAO said 40% of eligible claimants were automatically reinstated by November 2025, but HMRC couldn't provide average reinstatement time. Can you supply the range?\nJohn-Paul Marks: Yes—broadly the roll-out ran August–October, we paused and fixed by the end of November. Worst cases from August waited around three months. We'll write with fuller ranges and timings.\nChair: You paid £3,200 to 51 claimants—small sums overall. Were you making it hard to claim compensation, and do you pay interest on late payments?\nMyrtle Lloyd: We tried not to put barriers in the way. The payments were small because most people were reinstated and backdated promptly. The largest single redress was £300.\nSarah Olney: Can you confirm no one lost state pension entitlement because of suspensions?\nCerys McDonald: Yes—suspension did not terminate entitlement. Based on current information I can give the Committee an undertaking that state pension entitlement wasn’t affected; I will write if anything changes.\n\n\n## Governance, accountability and cross‑Government lessons\n> HMRC accepts it lacked sufficient senior oversight in the move to scale; it has implemented internal audit and NAO recommendations and is sharing lessons across Government via the Public Sector Fraud Authority.\n\nChair: NAO highlighted absence of a continuous senior responsible owner. Was that lacking here and has it been fixed?\nJohn-Paul Marks: Yes, NAO was right—SRO presence was insufficient in that phase. We’ve accepted the recommendation and now have clearer senior ownership, oversight and governance for scaling.\nRupert Lowe: You commissioned an internal audit—will you share its findings?\nJohn-Paul Marks: Yes. The internal audit informed our action plan and has been shared with the NAO and our audit committees. We’ll share outcomes as part of accountability.\nRob Malcomson: This is broadly an isolated incident but instructive. The Digital Economy Act review board, the PSFA and a community of practice will push hypercare and pre-deployment guardrails across Departments.\nJonathan Athow: We discussed the lessons at HMRC’s professional standards committee in 2026 to reassure colleagues we can innovate safely; we will use that governance route going forward.\nMr Betts: How do you stop staff becoming overly risk-averse after this?\nJohn-Paul Marks: We want a high appetite for responsible innovation—empowerment plus clear guardrails and senior oversight. The risk is lowering ambition; instead we aim for disciplined, tested scaling with customer safeguards.\nRob Malcomson: Bring policy, operations, legal and data governance together at design stage—co-design reduces surprises and allows safe innovation.\n\n\n## Next steps: data sources, debt recovery and public trust\n> HMRC will expand safe data use (student, schools, other travel data), proceed cautiously with new debt powers, and commit to more transparency and monitoring to maintain public trust.\n\nCatherine West: What additional data sources and nudges will HMRC use to spot changes in entitlement and latent unmet need?\nCerys McDonald: We already use school census, Student Loans Company and DWP feeds; we’ll add more targeted nudges (texts to parents) and use feeds to spot education or benefit claims changes quickly.\nRupert Lowe: Should claimants confirm eligibility annually?\nJohn-Paul Marks: We balance burden on 7 million families with targeted checks. There is already reconfirmation around age 16; we’ll improve nudges and tech to reduce burden while catching riskier cohorts.\nChair: About debt: the Chancellor announced powers for direct deductions. That’s draconian—how will you safeguard people before using it?\nJohn-Paul Marks: We’ll write to the Committee before December. Direct deduction will be a last resort, tested at low volume with senior oversight, refund-first safeguards and published controls to protect cashflow for vulnerable people.\nRob Malcomson: Better data quality—uniform identifiers and property/ residency data—would make these checks more accurate. Where data gaps remain, probabilistic matching plus follow-up contact must be used, not automatic action.\nChair: Thank you. The Committee will follow up for detailed timelines, reinstatement ranges, the internal audit outcomes and the promised letter on direct deduction safeguards.\n",
